WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.136
-I have many things
I want to discuss with you.

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.304
You know I love "Mobituaries"
and I like the podcast, as well.

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:06.406
And I want to talk about
"Fairycakes."

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.740
Let's just start with that.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.276
"Fairycakes" -- how did you get
involved with this?

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.344
And what part do you play?

00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:14.614
-Well, the playwright --
very funny writer --

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.482
Douglas Carter Beane,
called me and said,

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:20.553
"I've written a play that's
a mashup of classic stories like

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.922
'Pinocchio, 'Snow White,'
'Cinderella,'

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.091
and Shakespeare's
'A Midsummer Night's Dream.'

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.093
And I want you to play
Geppetto."

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:28.995
The Geppetto of "Pinocchio."

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:31.864
I was intrigued,
especially because this is not

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:34.000
the Geppetto
we grew up with, alright?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.502
Our grandparents' Geppetto.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.237
That Geppetto,
with all due respect,

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.873
was a failed wood-carver who
made terrible toys.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:42.341
He had no social life.

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:45.845
He spent all of his day chasing
around a sociopathic puppet.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:46.979
You know, the --
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:49.649
-And ends up being
swallowed by a whale.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.052
This is a vulnerable Geppetto,
a soulful Geppetto,

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:57.123
and a sexy Geppetto, so that's
what really drew me to the role.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.726
-Wow!
-Thank you.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.828
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:03.128
We are
remaking -- We are reclaiming

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:05.098
Geppetto on our terms.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.632
-Yeah. I also want to ask you,
because, always,

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:07.966
you have
something very interesting.

00:01:07.966 --> 00:01:09.769
Every time you come on,
we'll talk about something,

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.905
And you collect, like,
art history or something.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.173
So you collect some
old, cool things.

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:15.308
-Presidential memorabilia.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.610
-Yes, you love
presidential memorabilia,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.514
but you have a bust of
Grover Cleveland

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:22.515
in your house.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:23.783
-Well, right.

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:27.220
So, Grover Cleveland is, I mean,
by far my third-favorite

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.621
late-19th-century president.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:29.789
And so I had a bust of him --

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.357
-Don't pick favorites,
but, yeah, okay.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.726
-I had him in my apartment
for 20 years.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.662
And then I went
for "CBS Sunday Morning"

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:38.164
to do a story and I interviewed
the grandson of

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.799
Grover Cleveland,
George Cleveland.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:42.468
I showed him a picture of
my bust of what

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:43.936
I thought was Grover Cleveland,

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:46.071
and he said,
"That's not my grandfather."

00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:47.874
And I felt like -- It felt like

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.942
I was in one of those
Lifetime Network movies.

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:53.746
Like, this man that you thought
was a 19th century president

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.214
is not who you thought he was.

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:56.749
-Yeah. This is the bust
you have in your house.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.284
-This is right.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.719
For Christmas,
we put ornaments around him.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:01.087
-Yeah, of course,
you decorate it for Christmas.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:02.255
as one should, yeah.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.690
-Gigantic. And I went on --
And I thought,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.692
"If I only I could get on
'Antiques Roadshow.'"

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:09.362
And then they did these special
episodes during the pandemic,

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.631
and they invited me on.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.031
And I thought, "This is exactly
what I need them

00:02:13.031 --> 00:02:15.901
to help me figure out.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.737
And, anyway,
it's not worth a lot,

00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:19.939
as in it's worth nothing.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.076
It's just a big hunk of
painted plaster.

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:25.511
But Twitter did,
actually, a nice thing,

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:29.315
and after watching this,
a bunch of people on Twitter

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.352
sort of sent in clues
about who it was,

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:33.853
people that are
real history buffs.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:38.424
And it turns out that it is
a former New York --

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.694
NYPD police chief named
Richard Enright,

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.363
from the Prohibition era,
who had a great reputation,

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.498
kind of a badass.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.134
And, so, that's who --
That's who is --

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:49.902
He's now my security detail,
I guess.

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:52.672
-Wow! You're a big fan of him.
That's fantastic.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:53.772
See, but I love this --
All this stuff

00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:55.174
would only happen to you.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.377
I feel like you're just an --
You're an interesting guy,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.845
but you're interested in things.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.247
And so you go, "Yeah, so, I want
to find out what this is."

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.983
But that's what I like about
"Mobituaries."

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:05.652
Did this start as a podcast?

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:07.853
-It started as a pod--
It started as a podcast,

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:10.088
but I knew we were going to do
a book at the same time.

00:03:10.088 --> 00:03:12.524
The material is different for
the podcast and the book.

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:13.793
There's some overlap.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:17.530
But I just -- You know,
I went with subjects --

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.033
people and things -- that I feel
didn't get the sendoff

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:23.169
they deserved the first time.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.504
So there are
favorite entertainers in there,

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:28.174
like Sammy Davis Jr., who was
just, I think,

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:31.010
the greatest entertainer of
the 20th century, all around.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.978
My co-author,
Jonathan Greenberg, and I

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:34.914
also did "Mobituaries"

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.382
for sitcom characters
that disappeared.

00:03:37.382 --> 00:03:39.552
There is something called
the Chuck Cunningham Syndrome,

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:40.653
which you may know.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.322
-Now, who is Chuck --
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:45.191
-Thank you. He knows.
-No, I know who he was.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.360
Chuck -- Was it
Ron Howard's brother?

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.394
-Okay, so, yeah.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.429
So, Richie Cunningham,
Ron Howard,

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.198
had an older brother
for the first --

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:53.566
Oh, does this work?
-No, it doesn't.

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:55.334
-For the first two sea--
Oh, it does work.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:56.802
Wow!
-It does. Wow.

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:57.970
I never knew it worked.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.672
-It does.
-This is fantastic.

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:00.940
It's like we're doing
an old-timey radio show.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.441
-I know. I love it.
-Tell me about it.

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:04.043
-Bob and Ray.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:08.181
So, yes, Richie Cunningham had
an older brother,

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:11.483
Chuck Cunningham, on the first
few seasons of "Happy Days."

00:04:11.483 --> 00:04:12.818
He was known primarily for

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:14.820
dribbling a basketball
and eating a sandwich.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.589
He didn't really do much else.
-No.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.092
-The Fonz -- he became
more and more popular.

00:04:20.092 --> 00:04:21.994
And Garry Marshall,
the creator of the show,

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.696
who really was
kind of a genius, said,

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.598
"Look, why would
Richie Cunningham

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.700
go to anyone but
The Fonz for advice on

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.602
dating and, you know,
dealing in high school?"

00:04:30.602 --> 00:04:34.207
So, basically, Chuck Cunningham,
the older brother,

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.509
had to die for The Fonz to live.

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.444
And so they bas-- They just
disappeared the character.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:39.879
They never explained it.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:43.949
He just went upstairs one day
and just -- and never came down.

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:45.384
And the same thing happened to

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.286
Judy Winslow on
"Family Matters."

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:53.325
In February of 1993,
at Grandma Winslow's wedding,

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:55.827
which was
a very special episode,

00:04:55.827 --> 00:04:58.264
Judy Winslow --
that character --

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:01.300
she went down the aisle
as a flower girl

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.334
and then she disappeared.

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:03.635
She was never seen again.

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:05.237
-They didn't bring
her back at all.

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:07.373
-Right. And, really,
the blame lies with Urkel,

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:08.407
because Urkel --

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:09.775
-He got too famous.

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:12.577
-Urkel had become so big,
there just wasn't room

00:05:12.577 --> 00:05:13.880
for a cute little sister.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.981
So they had to vaporize her.

00:05:15.981 --> 00:05:17.150
-I love this.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.252
There is so many more
great, funny stories

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:20.452
and awesome stories in this.

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:21.787
"Mobituaries."

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:24.457
Please check out this book
and go check out Mo Rocca,

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:28.727
everybody, in "Fairycakes,"
running now through January 2nd

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:29.896
at the Greenwich House Theater!

